To keep your Ubuntu screen turned on permanently and prevent it from dimming, locking, or going to sleep, you can do this easily through either the graphical settings or the terminal.
## Method 1: Using the Graphical Settings (Easiest)

   1. Open the Settings application (click the top-right corner of your screen and select the gear icon).
   2. On the left sidebar, click on Power.
   3. Look for the Power Saving section:
   * Change Screen Blank to Never.
      * Change Automatic Suspend to Off.
   4. Next, click on Privacy in the left sidebar, then select Screen Lock.
   * Switch Automatic Screen Lock to Off.
   
------------------------------
## Method 2: Using the Terminal (Fastest)
If you are working via SSH or prefer using the command line, run these three commands to completely disable screen blanking, sleep, and locking:

# 1. Prevent the screen from blanking/turning off
gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.session idle-delay 0
# 2. Prevent the system from automatically suspending when idle
gsettings set org.gnome.settings-daemon.plugins.power sleep-inactive-ac-timeout 0
# 3. Disable the automatic screen lock
gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.screensaver lock-enabled false

(Note: Setting the values to 0 tells Ubuntu to never trigger the timeout.)
------------------------------
## Method 3: Using "Caffeine" (Best for Laptop Users)
If you only want your screen to stay on sometimes (like when you are running a long bug bounty script), you can install a lightweight tool called Caffeine. It adds a small coffee cup icon to your top bar that you can click to turn the screensaver on or off instantly.
To install it, run:

sudo apt update && sudo apt install caffeine -y

After installing, open your application menu, launch Caffeine Indicator, and click the toggle in your top panel.
